# Web-browser provider strings — English (source/fallback).

webbrowser-display-name = web browser
webbrowser-description = The web as a list: type an address, and the page comes back as headings, text, links and forms. Chrome renders it, off your screen.
webbrowser-setting-url-history = URL history

# A site can put a cookie choice in front of its content. It is answered on a
# page of its own; this introduces it.
webbrowser-step-consent = This site asks about cookies. Choose one to continue.
webbrowser-consent-unrecognised = This site is showing a cookie-consent page and no choice could be lifted out of it. The consent page itself is shown below.
webbrowser-consent-accept-all = Accept all cookies
webbrowser-consent-reject-all = Reject all cookies

# The language versions a site declares for itself, listed as the last section
# of the page. Not a question: the page is already there, this is one more part
# of it. The marker names the language you are reading right now.
webbrowser-languages = languages
webbrowser-language-current = current

# Bookmarks. The marker is prepended to a bookmarked row's display text, so it
# is read out before the address; keep it short and keep the brackets.
webbrowser-bookmark-marker = [bookmark]
webbrowser-bookmark-added = Bookmarked { $url }
webbrowser-bookmark-removed = Bookmark removed from { $url }
webbrowser-bookmark-nothing = No page to bookmark

# The tutorial's paragraphs about this program, under its programs section:
# <name>-tutorial, then <name>-tutorial-2 and so on, read until one is missing.
webbrowser-tutorial = Web browser, from the Store: type a URL in the address bar to load a page as a navigable tree of headings, links, and forms you can fill in. The cursor drops into the content once the page lands, and what it finds is grouped into navigation, main content, complementary, and footer, so you can step over a menu instead of reading through it.
webbrowser-tutorial-2 = Web addresses you have visited sit as rows under the address bar, newest first. Press Enter on one to load it again, and it moves back to the top. The list is saved, so it is still there the next time you start.
webbrowser-tutorial-3 = b: bookmark the history row you are on, or the page you are currently reading, even one you reached by following a link. Bookmarked rows are announced with [bookmark] in front, and they stay in the list once older addresses are dropped to keep it within its limit. Press b again to remove the bookmark.
webbrowser-tutorial-4 = Web browser commands: clear cookies signs you out of every site, and show hidden content reloads the page with nothing left out, which is the way back if a page reads oddly. A site that asks about its cookies puts that question first, as a step you answer before the content appears, and nothing is accepted on your behalf. A site offering several languages is not a question, its own language links are read as part of the page.
